---
import Article from '../../layouts/Article.astro'
import AssertionCard from '../../components/AssertionCard.astro'
import ChartFigure from '../../components/ChartFigure.astro'
import { assertion } from '../../lib/feeds'
import { bar } from '../../lib/chart-spec'

const overdose = assertion('overdose-deaths-fell-by-two-thirds')

const overdoseSpec = bar(
  overdose.figures.map((f) => f.label),
  overdose.figures.map((f) => f.value),
  { yTitle: 'Fall from peak', tickSuffix: '%', labelValues: true },
)
---

<Article slug="the-overdose-epidemic-ending">
  <p>
    One of those eleven rows has a second, faster source behind it. CDC publishes provisional
    county counts monthly, each a total for the twelve months ending that month, and Allen
    County's seventy-two windows are never suppressed.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={overdose} open />
  <ChartFigure
    id="overdose"
    title="Fall in drug overdose deaths from each peak, to December 2025"
    caption="Allen County's peak is the twelve months ending February 2023, Ohio's April 2021, the nation's June 2023 — so the three are measured from different months to the same endpoint. Ohio's figure is taken from CDC's state file rather than by summing its counties: the number of Ohio counties reporting falls from 73 to 45 across this period as counties drop below the suppression threshold, which would invent a decline."
    columns={['', 'Fall from peak']}
    rows={overdose.figures.map((f) => [f.label, `${f.value}%`])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="overdose" set:html={JSON.stringify(overdoseSpec)} is:inline />
  <p>
    What the fall does not do is close the gap above. The county's premature death rate is at its
    highest in 2020–2022, when overdoses were still rising, and this site holds no figure after
    2022 for deaths of any other kind. Whether the county's early deaths fell with its overdoses
    is not established.
  </p>
</Article>
